My original posting was:
>        One of my systems is down due to hardware problems.  I want to 
>have all incoming mail to this system switched over another system.  I'm 
>running nis and the users home and mail directories are nfs mounted to 
>all systems.  I thought all I needed to do was to change the mx record to 
>another system.  However the system that is now receiving the mail via 
>the mx record still won't accept the mail:
>
>Jan  4 13:38:29 uhhepj sendmail[12257]: AA12255: 
>     to=<vjs@uhheph.phys.hawaii.edu>, delay=00:01:16, 
>     stat=Deferred: Connection timed out during user open with 
>     uhheph.phys.hawaii.edu
>
>How do I make the system receiving the mail via the mx record(uhhepj), 
>simply receive the mail without trying to talk to down system (uhheph).
>I can think that a cname entry would work but that would give me another 
>set of problems when my down system is ready to boot.

Thanks to:
Doug Johnson <drjohn@pizero.Colorado.EDU>
Patrick O'Brien <pobrien@cfa.harvard.edu>
"Paul E. Rockwell" <rockwell@rch.dec.com>
Jim Belonis <belonis@dirac.phys.washington.edu>
Spider Boardman <spider@orb.nashua.nh.us>

I used Doug Johnson's suggestion below which worked!    
(added the next three lines to sendmail.cf)
Cwlocalhost
# file containing names of hosts for which we receive email
Fw/var/adm/sendmail/sendmail.cw

where /var/adm/sendmail/sendmail.cw would contain the hosts you want to
receive mail for:

host1.domain
host2.domain

other responses were similar:
You need to add the name uuheph to the Cw line in the
/var/adm/sendmail/sendmail.cf
eg.
Cwuhheph.phys.hawaii.edu

one response that I haven't tried was:
# Local (this subdomain) workstation mail that should be accepted here
# (requires MX records, too), e.g.
#CLhost1 host2
CLdirac

where 'dirac' is the name of the addressed machine.

Thank you,
